cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  decision-making-skills: agent skills for decisions under uncertainty, with an
  evaluation harness that measures whether they work
message: >-
  Cite the software entry below. Most published results measure whether a skill
  fires when it should; one five-arm study has measured decision quality, and no
  skill in this repository carries a verdict, because that study's target model
  is registered to a tier that emits none. SCORECARD.md holds the verdicts and
  is empty. The paper in `paper/` is complete and, as of 2026-09-01, submitted
  to nowhere yet; until it carries an arXiv identifier, cite the software.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Campa
    given-names: Angel
    alias: AngelCampa1
repository-code: https://github.com/AngelCampa1/decision-making-skills
url: https://angelcampa1.github.io/decision-making-skills/
abstract: >-
  A markdown Agent Skill that routes a decision to one of six procedures:
  evidence ledger, switching conditions, consequence cascade, decide or wait,
  council, and ask or decide. It reads only the one it routes to.
  Beside it is a Python evaluation harness (`decision_evals`) that measures
  whether such a skill changes behaviour. Runs are pre-registered, and the
  harness refuses to publish a result whose prediction cannot be shown by git
  ancestry to predate its data. A controlled design is written and enforced: a
  run compares a skill against an empty prompt, a word-count- and
  structure-matched placebo and plain chain-of-thought on the same items, so
  that a skill beating nothing while matching filler is reported as a length
  effect. `check_placebo_match` refuses a placebo of the wrong size or shape on
  every local gate run. One published run, the five-arm study of 2026-08-27,
  has used the placebo arm and an A/A control; no published run has used the
  chain-of-thought arm. Labels are re-derived blind by a panel
  of independent model instances, and the panel's agreement is reported
  chance-corrected (Fleiss' kappa, Krippendorff's alpha) alongside the raw
  rate. Statistics are exact and resampling-based, with templates as the
  resampling unit, and calibration goes through the Murphy decomposition.
  Most published results measure skill *triggering*, whether a skill fires when
  it should. Decision quality has one measurement, and it carries no verdict.
